WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.000
Welcome to the course today. I want to introduce today a technique for camera calibration.

00:07.000 --> 00:13.000
That means we want to estimate the intrinsic parameters, so the calibration matrix of our camera,

00:13.000 --> 00:15.000
not taking into account where the camera is.

00:15.000 --> 00:22.000
So in contrast to DLT, where we were estimating the intrinsics and the extrinsics at the same point in time,

00:22.000 --> 00:26.000
we are here trying to estimate only the intrinsics.

00:26.000 --> 00:33.000
Furthermore, we want to simplify our life a bit in the sense that we assume to know the 3D locations of control points in the environment

00:33.000 --> 00:35.000
that we needed for the DLT.

00:35.000 --> 00:43.000
And we will basically use here a checkerboard pattern, so a known printed 2D structure for performing the localization.

00:43.000 --> 00:47.000
And this approach, a method proposed by Zhang a bit more than 20 years ago,

00:47.000 --> 00:51.000
is today one of the standard methods for performing camera calibration

00:51.000 --> 00:55.000
and a lot of toolboxes that you use for performing camera calibration.

00:55.000 --> 00:59.000
Actually, he uses the ideas presented here.

00:59.000 --> 01:06.000
So if we look at the overall concept, we have that mapping that maps up from the world coordinate to the pixel coordinates

01:06.000 --> 01:08.000
and involve this projection matrix P.

01:08.000 --> 01:13.000
The projection matrix P contains the intrinsic parameters and the extrinsic parameters.

01:13.000 --> 01:16.000
That means the calibration parameters, which are the intrinsics,

01:16.000 --> 01:24.000
and the extrinsic parameters, which is the pose consisting of the position and orientation of a camera.

01:24.000 --> 01:28.000
And we are today only interested in the intrinsics, so we don't care where the camera is.

01:28.000 --> 01:32.000
We only want to find the intrinsic parameters of our camera.

01:32.000 --> 01:40.000
Again, the DLT mapping tells me how to project the point from the 3D world to 2D.

01:40.000 --> 01:44.000
And we have the image plane. We have our projection center here of our camera.

01:44.000 --> 01:50.000
We have the 3D point in the world that's projected onto the image plane and then goes into the camera.

01:50.000 --> 01:56.000
And for fully describing these mappings, we need to have P, which has 11 degrees of freedom.

01:56.000 --> 02:02.000
And DLT estimated all these 11 degrees of freedom, so calibration parameters here, the 5,

02:02.000 --> 02:05.000
and 3 translation, 3 rotational parameters.

02:05.000 --> 02:12.000
And today in Sanck's method, we don't care about R and X0 and we are only interested in getting the calibration matrix.

02:13.000 --> 02:18.000
So what I assume you need to know how that works, how to understand the lecture is,

02:18.000 --> 02:22.000
I assume you know how DLT works and how we compute the DLT.

02:22.000 --> 02:28.000
So if you don't know that, please go back and watch the lecture first on the direct linear transform

02:28.000 --> 02:34.000
and how to estimate the direct linear transform because we will basically reuse the solution for the DLT

02:34.000 --> 02:40.000
and then need to slightly modify it so that only the last step of the DLT is actually different for Sanck's method

02:40.000 --> 02:42.000
and the first part stays the same.

02:42.000 --> 02:47.000
So if you don't know the DLT, please watch the DLT lecture first. Thank you.

02:49.000 --> 02:56.000
So assuming you know how DLT works, we can now look into Sanck's method, which is a camera calibration approach,

02:56.000 --> 03:03.000
but this time it doesn't use known 3D points, 3D locations of at least 6 points in the environment.

03:03.000 --> 03:05.000
It uses a checkerboard pattern.

03:05.000 --> 03:07.000
So what you see here is an image of a checkerboard pattern.

03:07.000 --> 03:15.000
This is basically a printout of a black-white pattern, which we assume to know, that you can glue on a flat surface

03:15.000 --> 03:20.000
and then, let's say, a piece of wood or cardboard or whatever it is, it only should be flat,

03:20.000 --> 03:22.000
and then you can show this checkerboard in front of your camera.

03:22.000 --> 03:29.000
So you basically dance around with a checkerboard in front of your camera and generate images of this checkerboard pattern.

03:29.000 --> 03:36.000
And under the assumption that you know the checkerboard and you also know that it's a flat surface,

03:36.000 --> 03:41.000
that's something that we can actually exploit because we're now observing a known structure

03:41.000 --> 03:47.000
without actually measuring 3D points in the environment as it was the case for the DLT.

03:48.000 --> 03:50.000
So we assume to know this checkerboard.

03:50.000 --> 03:53.000
Let's say we have observation of this checkerboard from different sizes.

03:53.000 --> 03:55.000
This is one example over here.

03:55.000 --> 03:59.000
And we assume to know the size and the structure of the checkerboard,

03:59.000 --> 04:04.000
and one of the important things to exploit in here is that the checkerboard is a flat surface.

04:04.000 --> 04:10.000
It is one plane and this is a key property that we are actually going to exploit.

04:10.000 --> 04:16.000
So what we can do is we typically simply run, let's say, a corner detector on that checkerboard

04:16.000 --> 04:21.000
so you know how that works, finding interest points or key points so that you actually find the corner points of this checkerboard.

04:21.000 --> 04:27.000
So you get a large number of point observations and you at least know where those points are on the checkerboard.

04:28.000 --> 04:33.000
So what you do not know is where the 3D location of those checkerpoints are in the 3D world,

04:33.000 --> 04:38.000
but what you know is where those points are on the checkerboard.

04:38.000 --> 04:41.000
So this is something that we can assume to know.

04:41.000 --> 04:43.000
And then we actually use a trick.

04:43.000 --> 04:47.000
And we say for every image we define an own coordinate system.

04:47.000 --> 04:53.000
And the coordinate system is defined in a way that the xy-plane forms as the checkerboard.

04:53.000 --> 04:59.000
So every time the checkerboard defines the xy-plane.

05:00.000 --> 05:10.000
And we can exploit this fact for defining the xyz locations of all those points here in this coordinate frame.

05:10.000 --> 05:14.000
Of course it is important to know that coordinate frame changes from frame to frame,

05:14.000 --> 05:17.000
but within a frame we have the same coordinate system.

05:17.000 --> 05:25.000
And often this is sufficient, not often, in this approach this is sufficient in order to actually estimate the calibration parameters of our camera.

05:25.000 --> 05:31.000
So the trick is that all points lie on the checkerboard and lie which defines the xy-plane of our coordinate system.

05:31.000 --> 05:37.000
That means that all the coordinates in the 3D world have a z-coordinate of zero.

05:37.000 --> 05:40.000
So all those points here have a z-coordinate of zero.

05:40.000 --> 05:42.000
All those points here have a z-coordinate of zero.

05:42.000 --> 05:45.000
And all those points here have a z-coordinate of zero.

05:45.000 --> 05:51.000
Just because for every frame we define an own coordinate system, well this is actually the case.

05:51.000 --> 05:59.000
So this exploits that the checkerboard is a flat, is a plane, so it's glued on a flat surface.

05:59.000 --> 06:01.000
And this is kind of a key thing we exploit.

06:01.000 --> 06:08.000
And then we know the xy locations as well because we know the size of the printout that we use for generating this checkerboard.

06:09.000 --> 06:17.000
Okay, so let's see how this assumption that z equals to zero for all 3D points changes the mass of the overall system.

06:17.000 --> 06:19.000
And it does it substantially.

06:19.000 --> 06:23.000
So this was the mapping that we used to describe.

06:23.000 --> 06:27.000
So this is our homogeneous 3D coordinate vector.

06:27.000 --> 06:32.000
This is the matrix containing the rotational part and the translational part.

06:32.000 --> 06:38.000
And here we then have the calibration matrix and this maps it to our 2D pixel coordinate.

06:38.000 --> 06:44.000
So now we know that the z-coordinate of all points are zero.

06:44.000 --> 06:46.000
So each point has a z-coordinate of zero.

06:46.000 --> 06:51.000
So this red-circled variable is zero all the time.

06:51.000 --> 06:52.000
Okay?

06:52.000 --> 06:57.000
So now if we multiply this matrix by this point, knowing that this point is zero,

06:57.000 --> 07:04.000
it actually turns out that this column over here has a zero effect on our system because it will be always taken down to zero.

07:04.000 --> 07:07.000
So it's something that I don't need to estimate.

07:07.000 --> 07:12.000
That means the last column of my rotation matrix is something I don't need here.

07:12.000 --> 07:24.000
So this is not needed, so we can actually eliminate those variables from our equation system

07:24.000 --> 07:30.000
because this is the equation system relating our observation with our knowledge about our model.

07:30.000 --> 07:38.000
So by eliminating this column and eliminating the third row of this vector over here, we turn into an equation which looks like this.

07:38.000 --> 07:39.000
Okay?

07:40.000 --> 07:46.000
So a point on the checkerboard, the x, y-coordinate, is mapped to the image coordinate

07:46.000 --> 07:51.000
and I don't need to know the z-coordinate of that point in the 3D world

07:51.000 --> 07:55.000
because I know it's zero for that own reference system.

07:55.000 --> 08:04.000
So every point on my checkerboard will generate me one of those equations, right?

08:04.000 --> 08:08.000
So every point in the 3D world on that checkerboard generates me this equation

08:08.000 --> 08:16.000
and the parameters in here and here, so these parameters are the same for all images and all checkerboard observations

08:16.000 --> 08:24.000
and this matrix over here is the same for every frame, so all the points which have been extracted from the same checkerboard image.

08:24.000 --> 08:28.000
Okay? So that's important to differentiate. We'll need that later on.

08:28.000 --> 08:37.000
Okay, so we have a matrix H which is now a 3x3 matrix, so the multiplication of those two matrices over here,

08:37.000 --> 08:49.000
which I can write as three column vectors H1, H2, H3, so this is something that I can split up into my calibration matrix K

08:49.000 --> 08:56.000
and my matrix which now consists of two vectors R1 and R2 which are the first two columns of my rotation matrix

08:56.000 --> 09:04.000
and then my translation vector T over here, so that one point generates me this equation over here.

09:04.000 --> 09:08.000
So I have multiple points, for now we're just considering one single image,

09:08.000 --> 09:12.000
so all the points from the checkerboard that are generated for one single image,

09:12.000 --> 09:15.000
so for now just one single image is taken into account.

09:15.000 --> 09:23.000
So we get a number of those equations. If we have i points on our checkerboard, we actually obtain those, this equation over here

09:23.000 --> 09:30.000
and again these are knowns, these are knowns and my unknowns actually sit in here

09:31.000 --> 09:34.000
and now it's important to know about DLT.

09:34.000 --> 09:39.000
So if you haven't watched the video on DLT, I suggest to do that before you continue here

09:39.000 --> 09:46.000
because knowing how to solve this requires you to have seen the DLT lecture.

09:46.000 --> 09:52.000
So we can perform this step exactly as the first steps of the DLT,

09:52.000 --> 09:57.000
actually every step except the last step of the DLT we can apply in exactly the same way.

09:57.000 --> 10:03.000
So if you remember that we said we have our knowns here and our unknowns in here, what do we do?

10:03.000 --> 10:11.000
We vectorize this matrix H so that it contains our nine parameters, H11 to H33,

10:11.000 --> 10:21.000
we stack them into a vector and then we compute the corresponding coefficients from lowercase x and uppercase x,

10:21.000 --> 10:25.000
one's the image coordinates and the other one the 3D world coordinates.

10:25.000 --> 10:34.000
So that instead of having the 3x4 projection matrix P as in the DLT, we now have a 3x3 homography,

10:34.000 --> 10:41.000
this is our matrix H, so that we then have exactly these coefficients for my H,

10:41.000 --> 10:49.000
so this H are kind of the nine stacked values, so it's nine dimensional here, not twelve dimensional as for the DLT,

10:49.000 --> 10:51.000
but the rest stays the same.

10:51.000 --> 10:55.000
Just to show you the correspondency between the problem that we have here and the DLT problem,

10:55.000 --> 11:03.000
I wrote here the coefficient vectors down for the DLT and crossed out all those which go away

11:03.000 --> 11:11.000
because the z-coordinate of all points are zero, so they are eliminated and I just don't need to build up these twelve elements here,

11:11.000 --> 11:18.000
I just need to go down to the nine elements that I need because the others I eliminated already from my equation system.

11:19.000 --> 11:29.000
So it's the same as in the DLT except that we can cross out those three columns or rows from the coefficient vector.

11:29.000 --> 11:35.000
And as a result of this we get a kind of slightly simplified form where it just kind of now crossed out

11:35.000 --> 11:39.000
or eliminated all the variables that were crossed out in the previous slide.

11:39.000 --> 11:46.000
So then we have my unknowns, my coefficient vectors, I get those coefficient vectors

11:47.000 --> 11:53.000
and then I need to solve the resulting system of linear equations in order to estimate H.

11:53.000 --> 12:02.000
And we can do this using the singular value decomposition as in the same way as we did that for the DLT estimation that we have done.

12:02.000 --> 12:09.000
So we may need to think about how many points do we actually need in order to estimate this H.

12:09.000 --> 12:18.000
So for the DLT, remember, we required overall six different points, but now here the situation is slightly different.

12:18.000 --> 12:24.000
So we need to have at least four points to determine H. Why is this the case?

12:24.000 --> 12:30.000
Because H has eight degrees of freedom, it's a three by three matrix, so it's nine elements, but again it's a homogeneous matrix,

12:30.000 --> 12:35.000
so it only has eight degrees of freedom because it's only defined up to a scaling factor.

12:35.000 --> 12:44.000
Every point on the checkerboard gives us two equations, two observations, an X observation and a Y observation on my sensor,

12:44.000 --> 12:48.000
so on pixel location X in X and the pixel location in Y.

12:48.000 --> 12:57.000
And so that means we need to have at least four points in the checkerboard in order to do this in order to provide an estimate for H.

12:57.000 --> 13:08.000
And again, this is now image dependent, so it is dependent per image because we defined the coordinate system independent for every image based on the location of the checkerboard.

13:08.000 --> 13:18.000
And this provides me an estimate for H, and after we have computed using the singular value decomposition exactly as we did it for the DLT,

13:18.000 --> 13:27.000
we actually come up with an estimate for H. So we have H now, and now the question is how do I get K from H?

13:27.000 --> 13:35.000
And this is the point in time where things change between Tsang's method and the original DLT estimation that we discussed.

13:35.000 --> 13:45.000
So just as a reminder, what have we done in the DLT? In the DLT we also had this matrix P that we need to decompose into matrices.

13:45.000 --> 13:57.000
It was actually not fully the matrix P, but the first 3x3 block out of my matrix that I cut out of my matrix P that I needed to decompose into a rotation matrix and a calibration matrix.

13:57.000 --> 14:03.000
And for that I used the QR decomposition because the QR decomposition was exactly what I wanted to do.

14:03.000 --> 14:11.000
It generates for me a rotation matrix and upper triangular matrix or lower triangular matrix that I can actually generate out of that.

14:11.000 --> 14:18.000
And this was exactly what I needed in order to obtain my K and my rotation matrix.

14:18.000 --> 14:24.000
Now unfortunately the story is different because this thing here is not a rotation matrix anymore.

14:24.000 --> 14:31.000
Because we have the translation vector key sitting in here which basically replaced the third column of my rotation matrix.

14:31.000 --> 14:36.000
And this is the problem why we can't apply the QR decomposition.

14:36.000 --> 14:43.000
So this is now the point in time where the DLT and Sankt's method basically deviate and need to generate two different solutions.

14:43.000 --> 14:52.000
And it actually gets a little bit more complicated here for Sankt's method because we cannot simply apply QR decomposition in such an elegant way as we did this for the DLT.

14:52.000 --> 14:55.000
And this is what makes it more tricky.

14:55.000 --> 14:59.000
So the question is how do we now obtain our matrix decomposition?

14:59.000 --> 15:04.000
So even if I don't care about this part here, I can ignore this part but actually get K.

15:04.000 --> 15:07.000
So I'm not interested in this part but I want to get K.

15:07.000 --> 15:13.000
And so if I have my matrix H, I need to go from H to K.

15:13.000 --> 15:17.000
So this is the goal, estimating K from H.

15:17.000 --> 15:24.000
So again the problem, we computed H via SVD and now we need to extract K.

15:24.000 --> 15:27.000
We need to break up this matrix H into two matrices.

15:27.000 --> 15:37.000
One is this calibration matrix, this upper triangular matrix and this second matrix where I only know that the first two columns are actually columns from a rotation matrix.

15:37.000 --> 15:45.000
Now to do this there is kind of not a standard decomposition that I can use in order to exploit those properties so that's something that I now need to do by hand.

15:45.000 --> 15:51.000
By hand means manually exploiting the constraints of things that I know for example about R1 and R2.

15:51.000 --> 15:55.000
And so I need to now derive an own solution to do this.

15:55.000 --> 15:58.000
And this is actually a procedure consisting of four steps.

15:58.000 --> 16:03.000
So for the first thing we need to do, we need to write down certain constraints that we know.

16:03.000 --> 16:09.000
So for example what do I know about K, what do I know about R1, what do I know about R2, how can I actually exploit this?

16:09.000 --> 16:15.000
And I will exploit properties that I knew that R1 and R2 are column vectors from a rotation matrix.

16:15.000 --> 16:31.000
You know the columns of a rotation matrix have certain constraints and I can exploit those constraints, write them down in order to form a system of equations that I then need to solve and then be hopefully able to decompose the matrices.

16:31.000 --> 16:35.000
So first I will write down the constraints and exploit constraints.

16:35.000 --> 16:44.000
Then I will define a matrix B which is K inverse transposed times K inverse.

16:44.000 --> 16:53.000
We'll see that this term K inverse transposed K inverse is a term that pops up in a couple of the equations that I actually write down.

16:53.000 --> 16:59.000
And so I define this matrix B so that my math gets slightly more similar.

16:59.000 --> 17:09.000
And then I will, I can find, I have to define a system of equations and this system of equations will actually lead me to B.

17:09.000 --> 17:22.000
So by solving a matrix, by solving the system that I derived from the constraints I formulated here in number 1, I will be able to estimate the elements of B.

17:22.000 --> 17:31.000
And once I have the elements of D, I need to decompose B so that I can break down B and obtain K.

17:31.000 --> 17:34.000
So these are kind of multiple steps I'm doing.

17:34.000 --> 17:49.000
I'm defining constraints, I'm defining a matrix B, I'm then solving my constraints so that I can determine the elements of B and then I do a matrix decomposition, actually a Cholesky decomposition of B so that I then obtain my matrix K.

17:49.000 --> 18:00.000
So there are a couple of steps in between and a number of coefficients that I actually need to compute in this process but this then allows me to decompose this matrix B.

18:00.000 --> 18:04.000
But the key thing to start with is to understand are those constraints.

18:04.000 --> 18:10.000
What are the constraints? Which constraints can I actually exploit from K, R1 and R2?

18:10.000 --> 18:17.000
So again, this was the equation that I have, my calibration matrix here and this remaining matrix R.

18:17.000 --> 18:20.000
So what do I know about that?

18:20.000 --> 18:27.000
The first thing I know, what I can actually do is I know that my matrix K is actually an invertible matrix.

18:27.000 --> 18:40.000
So it's this upper triangular matrix and all the elements on the main diagonal are unequal to zero so I know that K is invertible and I can invert my matrix K.

18:40.000 --> 18:43.000
This is a property that I'm exploiting now.

18:43.000 --> 18:47.000
What I can do is I can invert my matrix K and multiply it from the left hand side of this equation.

18:47.000 --> 18:56.000
So if I multiply to the left hand side of this equation I have K inverse times H and this gives me my matrix R1, R2, T.

18:56.000 --> 19:08.000
So this is basically what I've written down here, R1, R2, T is K inverse times the three column vectors of H that I have.

19:08.000 --> 19:11.000
So H, remember H is known, so this H I do know.

19:11.000 --> 19:16.000
What I don't know is K and what I don't know are those parameters here.

19:16.000 --> 19:28.000
But what I also know is from this equation that R1 is nothing else than K inverse times this vector because this gives me exactly the first element of this equation.

19:28.000 --> 19:30.000
The same for the second one.

19:30.000 --> 19:35.000
So R2 is K inverse R2 and for T I could do the same but I don't need that for the moment.

19:35.000 --> 19:37.000
I only need this for R1 and R2.

19:37.000 --> 19:43.000
So I can express R1 as K inverse times H1 and K inverse H2.

19:43.000 --> 19:50.000
And if I would know K, I could compute R but if I would know K I wouldn't have a problem in here, I wouldn't need to continue.

19:50.000 --> 19:53.000
So the problem is I can't actually compute this right now.

19:53.000 --> 19:59.000
But I know that these equality exist so they must be equal to R1 and R2.

19:59.000 --> 20:05.000
But what I then can do is I can exploit the fact that R1 and R2 stem from a rotation matrix.

20:05.000 --> 20:08.000
So I know certain things about this rotation matrix.

20:08.000 --> 20:13.000
So actually R1, R2, R3, although R3 is not relevant here, form an orthonormal basis.

20:13.000 --> 20:15.000
That means two things.

20:15.000 --> 20:22.000
First, R1 transposed times R2 must be zero because those two vectors are orthogonal to each other.

20:22.000 --> 20:25.000
So if I compute the dot product, they should be zero.

20:25.000 --> 20:31.000
And the second thing that I know is that R1 and R2 are of unit length, so have length one.

20:31.000 --> 20:36.000
So these are two constraints that I can actually derive, extract from this.

20:36.000 --> 20:43.000
By knowing that R1 and R2 stem from a rotation matrix, so that R1 transposed R2 must be zero

20:43.000 --> 20:47.000
and the norm of R1 and the norm of R2 is equal and they equal to one.

20:47.000 --> 20:50.000
And this is now something that I can exploit.

20:50.000 --> 20:55.000
So I use this one over here, this equation, these two equations actually,

20:55.000 --> 21:05.000
and these two equations over here to relate K and H using those constraints down here.

21:05.000 --> 21:11.000
So what I do is I just take the constraint over here, take R1 from here,

21:11.000 --> 21:20.000
and put this K inverse H in for A1 and K inverse H2 for R2 and note that this is zero.

21:20.000 --> 21:22.000
This is basically what happened over here.

21:22.000 --> 21:25.000
So this were two equations I had before.

21:25.000 --> 21:30.000
I use R1 transposed R2 equals zero, so I just put that in.

21:30.000 --> 21:33.000
So this is H1 transposed, K inverse transposed.

21:33.000 --> 21:39.000
So K minus to the power of minus T means the inverse of K being transposed

21:39.000 --> 21:43.000
and this is K inverse H2 must be zero.

21:43.000 --> 21:50.000
So it's something that follows directly from the fact that I know that R was a rotation matrix.

21:50.000 --> 21:55.000
And so the second thing I can do now, I exploit the second constraint

21:55.000 --> 21:59.000
naming that R1 and R2 are vectors of length one.

21:59.000 --> 22:04.000
And so I know they must be equal and they must have length one.

22:04.000 --> 22:17.000
So I can actually write down R1 is nothing or R1, the norm of R1 is R1 transposed times R1.

22:17.000 --> 22:24.000
So it turns into H1 transposed, K inverse transposed, K inverse H1.

22:24.000 --> 22:28.000
So not two in here, here was a two that would be zero but it's one.

22:28.000 --> 22:35.000
So this must give me length one and I know that this must be equal to the same thing done for R2.

22:35.000 --> 22:38.000
So this is the second constraint that I have.

22:38.000 --> 22:41.000
What I can now do is I can just move that to the other side

22:41.000 --> 22:44.000
so I again get an equation where this is equal to zero.

22:44.000 --> 22:49.000
So again here I just move these parts to the other side, it's equal to zero.

22:49.000 --> 22:54.000
So this equation, these two equations are now constraints that I formulated

22:54.000 --> 23:02.000
which relate me elements that I know which are my H vectors, their unknowns

23:02.000 --> 23:08.000
with my unknowns which are here my Ks in an equation that equals to zero.

23:08.000 --> 23:13.000
And this is something that I will exploit in a second.

23:13.000 --> 23:18.000
So the next thing is what I can do now if I kind of write down the equations under each other

23:18.000 --> 23:25.000
I see that this K inverse transposed times K inverse appears in all of my equations.

23:25.000 --> 23:31.000
So just kind of, what I can do is I can just define the symmetric and positive matrix B

23:31.000 --> 23:36.000
which has the form K inverse transposed, K inverse.

23:36.000 --> 23:42.000
So it basically can replace this thing by these, just a variable replacement for now.

23:42.000 --> 23:46.000
And then I have here the form that I have my known vectors over here

23:46.000 --> 23:51.000
and my unknown matrix sitting there in the middle.

23:51.000 --> 23:59.000
And by knowing that B consists of a matrix transposed times a matrix

23:59.000 --> 24:04.000
I can actually see that there exists a standard matrix decomposition

24:04.000 --> 24:09.000
that actually breaks me a matrix up, that is symmetric and positive definite

24:09.000 --> 24:13.000
into a matrix times the matrix of its transposed.

24:13.000 --> 24:15.000
So this is the Cholesky decomposition.

24:15.000 --> 24:21.000
So the Cholesky decomposition of an arbitrary matrix breaks it up into two matrices

24:21.000 --> 24:28.000
of two triangular matrices and the one if the other one transpose.

24:28.000 --> 24:33.000
So if I have B I can compute the Cholesky decomposition from B

24:33.000 --> 24:40.000
and it gives me the matrix A so that A times A transposed gives me my matrix B.

24:40.000 --> 24:45.000
So this is a decomposition that we typically use for solving our linear systems in an efficient way.

24:45.000 --> 24:52.000
But I can also use it here, so if I say A is K inverse transposed

24:52.000 --> 24:57.000
and I put it in here, then the product will give me B

24:57.000 --> 25:02.000
and if I can turn B into A A transpose into A using the Cholesky decomposition

25:02.000 --> 25:06.000
I can directly derive the calibration matrix out of it.

25:06.000 --> 25:09.000
The only thing I need to know is I need to compute the inverse and invert it

25:09.000 --> 25:13.000
but that's something which is easy with a three by three matrix.

25:13.000 --> 25:19.000
So the key insight in here, if we know B then we can directly compute K.

25:19.000 --> 25:25.000
So exploiting the Cholesky decomposition we can turn B into K.

25:25.000 --> 25:31.000
The problem is we don't have B yet but for the next step we'll focus on how to actually get B.

25:31.000 --> 25:36.000
And for that we will exploit these equations over here.

25:36.000 --> 25:45.000
So what we have is, if we inspect the equations we have B as a matrix which consists of our unknowns

25:45.000 --> 25:51.000
and given that the symmetric matrix we only have six unknowns not nine unknowns

25:51.000 --> 25:56.000
and H are our knowns and we have two equations that relate B and H with each other

25:56.000 --> 25:59.000
and actually sets them equal to zero.

25:59.000 --> 26:03.000
And this is something that we actually have seen in the past quite often.

26:03.000 --> 26:08.000
So also in the DLT lecture you have seen that we got an equation of a similar form

26:08.000 --> 26:14.000
that we exploited to compute, to reformulate it so that we have a coefficient vector

26:14.000 --> 26:21.000
times an unknown vector equals to zero and we can actually do this if we have this form of equation over here.

26:21.000 --> 26:26.000
And so we'll use basically now the same trick again of using this, exploiting the SVD

26:26.000 --> 26:33.000
setting up a system of linear equations that we are going to solve in a very similar way as we used it for the DLT

26:33.000 --> 26:37.000
and can use this to compute the matrix B.

26:37.000 --> 26:42.000
So again we have our matrix B which consists of six unknowns

26:42.000 --> 26:45.000
given that the symmetric matrix only need to care about those six unknowns

26:45.000 --> 26:56.000
so B can be described by my vector lowercase B contains the six unknowns of my matrix uppercase.

26:56.000 --> 27:00.000
And again this is also something which is only defined up to a scale factor.

27:00.000 --> 27:05.000
And then I have the constraints that I have written down before

27:05.000 --> 27:07.000
so the two constraints that you've seen before

27:07.000 --> 27:14.000
I can actually reformulate it with a coefficient which is now a vector v

27:14.000 --> 27:17.000
so that v transpose times B equals to zero

27:17.000 --> 27:25.000
and a second one where v transpose times B minus different v times B equals zero

27:25.000 --> 27:29.000
so these are basically the coefficients which result from this equation

27:29.000 --> 27:32.000
and the coefficients which result from this equation over here.

27:32.000 --> 27:37.000
And so again similar as I did it for the DLT

27:37.000 --> 27:40.000
can set up one constraint which looks like this

27:40.000 --> 27:43.000
and a second constraint which looks like this

27:43.000 --> 27:50.000
and now you can see that this thing actually comes much much closer to the thing how we used the DLT

27:50.000 --> 27:55.000
for solving this homogeneous system in order to derive our unknowns.

27:55.000 --> 28:01.000
We can do now the same thing so we can actually set up a matrix capital V which is defined in this form

28:01.000 --> 28:05.000
so putting the coefficients, stacking the coefficients together

28:05.000 --> 28:10.000
and also you can actually write that down vij as of this form

28:10.000 --> 28:12.000
and these are all the elements of my matrix H

28:12.000 --> 28:16.000
so if you actually want to write that down and implement it you need to know this matrix here

28:16.000 --> 28:20.000
but they can be directly derived from the constraint that we had before.

28:20.000 --> 28:24.000
So that for every image we obtain this matrix here

28:24.000 --> 28:28.000
so this is a matrix and this is B

28:28.000 --> 28:31.000
so this is a two times six matrix over here

28:31.000 --> 28:35.000
and this is a six dimensional vector, this equals to zero.

28:35.000 --> 28:40.000
So for every image I can generate these constraints in here

28:40.000 --> 28:44.000
which must hold for my matrix K

28:44.000 --> 28:48.000
or for my matrix B which I then use to compute K to be precise.

28:48.000 --> 28:54.000
So for one image I can compute my H

28:54.000 --> 29:01.000
and then from this H compute this coefficient vector exactly using this equation over here.

29:01.000 --> 29:08.000
And then the problem is this is just a two by six matrix

29:08.000 --> 29:13.000
so I need to stack multiple images together at least three so that I would get a six by six matrix

29:13.000 --> 29:19.000
but we can take more images so if I take n images I get a 2n by six matrix

29:19.000 --> 29:26.000
where the first two dimensions relate to image one, dimension three and four to image two and so on and so forth

29:26.000 --> 29:32.000
so that in the end image 2n minus one and 2n give me the information from image number n

29:32.000 --> 29:36.000
multiplied with my six dimensional vector and this must be equal to zero

29:36.000 --> 29:39.000
and this is something that I need to solve.

29:39.000 --> 29:47.000
Solving a linear system of homogeneous form to obtain my unknown vector B

29:47.000 --> 29:51.000
which then will fill me the elements of my matrix B

29:51.000 --> 29:56.000
so that I can then using the Cholesky decomposition turn that into K.

29:56.000 --> 29:58.000
So how do I solve such a system?

29:58.000 --> 30:03.000
V times B equals zero which is not the trivial solution

30:03.000 --> 30:06.000
so we impose this additional constraint that B equals to one

30:06.000 --> 30:11.000
and then we need to minimize the equation with the norm

30:11.000 --> 30:15.000
exactly in the same way as we have done it for the DLT in the DLT lecture using the SVD.

30:15.000 --> 30:19.000
So what we are doing, we are computing the singular value decomposition

30:19.000 --> 30:25.000
and then taking out the singular vector that corresponds to the minimum singular value

30:25.000 --> 30:30.000
which should be zero but is not zero in reality as real measurements are typically noisy

30:30.000 --> 30:35.000
so we take the singular value which has the smallest value

30:35.000 --> 30:40.000
so the last one in this diagonal matrix that is generated from the singular value decomposition

30:41.000 --> 30:48.000
take out the corresponding singular vector which then is the solution to B

30:48.000 --> 30:55.000
so then I obtain my B star which is the singular vector that minimizes this equation

30:55.000 --> 30:59.000
because this is the one which minimizes the term over here.

30:59.000 --> 31:07.000
Okay, then I have B and then I just compute my Cholesky decomposition and obtain out my matrix K.

31:07.000 --> 31:09.000
So just to sum up again, what's needed in here?

31:09.000 --> 31:13.000
We need our checkerboard images and we need to have at least three of those images.

31:13.000 --> 31:18.000
We will extract points out of all those checkerboards

31:18.000 --> 31:23.000
and we were exploiting that we know the size of the checkerboard and we know it's a flat surface

31:23.000 --> 31:27.000
and from four points we can compute our matrix H

31:27.000 --> 31:31.000
and the matrix H is the individual matrix H for every image

31:31.000 --> 31:37.000
so we have an own H for this image, an own H for that image and an own H for that image over here.

31:38.000 --> 31:45.000
Okay, so H is computed for every image and then each of the plane

31:45.000 --> 31:51.000
so basically each image gives us two equations for computing B.

31:51.000 --> 31:57.000
So for this B we got two equations out of every image.

31:57.000 --> 32:04.000
Since B has five or six degrees of freedom we need three different views of that plane.

32:04.000 --> 32:13.000
Okay, so this is because B was a symmetric matrix but it's only known up to a scaling coefficient

32:13.000 --> 32:15.000
so therefore we have in the end five degrees of freedom

32:15.000 --> 32:24.000
but that means we need three different views for three different images in order to compute the matrix B

32:24.000 --> 32:30.000
and then I need to solve V times B equals to zero with singular value decomposition

32:30.000 --> 32:35.000
and then compute my K out of the Cholesky decomposition.

32:35.000 --> 32:40.000
So you've seen here that there were a couple of steps actually needed in order to make this decomposition

32:40.000 --> 32:43.000
so we basically proceeded S for estimating the DLT

32:43.000 --> 32:48.000
but then we had to do some extra work because the matrix we wanted to decompose

32:48.000 --> 32:53.000
had not this nice form of being upper triangular matrix and rotation matrix

32:53.000 --> 32:56.000
the properties of the rotation matrix didn't hold

32:56.000 --> 33:02.000
we only knew that for two of the columns that they stem from a rotation matrix

33:02.000 --> 33:04.000
and thus we needed to do this extra work here

33:04.000 --> 33:08.000
and then also needed more images because requiring more than one image

33:08.000 --> 33:13.000
was something that came from the constraint that we have derived in here

33:13.000 --> 33:17.000
so therefore we needed three images not only one image

33:17.000 --> 33:29.000
because this constraint didn't hold that the second matrix that I need to decompose is a rotation matrix

33:29.000 --> 33:31.000
this is extra work that needed to be done

33:31.000 --> 33:37.000
but with this extra work we are actually able to do this decomposition in a manual form

33:37.000 --> 33:41.000
and this allows me now to compute my camera calibration matrix

33:41.000 --> 33:45.000
at least the five parameters that are typically in there

33:45.000 --> 33:48.000
or which describe the linear parameters that are involved

33:48.000 --> 33:52.000
and which allows me the kind of the first step of my camera calibration

33:52.000 --> 33:56.000
so you may ask yourself now what's with the nonlinear parameters

33:56.000 --> 34:00.000
so if you think about the lecture on describing the mapping X equals PX

34:00.000 --> 34:04.000
we also talked briefly about the nonlinear parameters

34:04.000 --> 34:10.000
so what we typically have in this setup is we have an additional or we have a calibration matrix

34:10.000 --> 34:14.000
which is multiplied with an additional term here from left hand side

34:14.000 --> 34:20.000
which is this kind of shift in the X and Y which is location dependent

34:20.000 --> 34:24.000
so this correction which takes into account the nonlinear errors

34:24.000 --> 34:27.000
we haven't taken into account on it in the past there

34:27.000 --> 34:32.000
takes the pixel location in the image plane into account

34:32.000 --> 34:36.000
in order to compute basically a shift depending on the pixel location

34:36.000 --> 34:38.000
and based on some unknown parameters

34:38.000 --> 34:43.000
and this is multiplied to the calibration matrix K to take into account the nonlinear errors

34:43.000 --> 34:46.000
things I could do with it is to take into account lens distortion

34:46.000 --> 34:52.000
like the barrel distortion over here or kind of the inverse one or combinations of both

34:52.000 --> 34:58.000
and I need to have those additional corrections in order to take lens distortion like those

34:58.000 --> 35:00.000
or even more complex ones into account

35:00.000 --> 35:03.000
